        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle on the Ice</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">5 April 1242</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The frozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peipus">Lake Peipus</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle">Novgorod First Chronicle</a> names Uzmen, by the Raven's Rock. Where on the lake, or beside it, is disputed</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">Novgorod and Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, around 5,000 men by David Nicolle's conservative estimate; the Livonian Order and the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Hermann of Dorpat, around 2,600 by the same estimate</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Alexander's forces won. The Livonian Rhymed Chronicle gives twenty Brothers dead and six captured; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle">Novgorod First Chronicle</a> gives 400 Germans dead and fifty taken, with a countless number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> killed.</span></div>
