    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Estimates of the armies vary widely among scholars; David Nicolle's conservative figures are around 5,000 Novgorodians against around 2,600 crusaders.</li><li>None of the primary sources says the ice broke or that knights drowned; Donald Ostrowski concludes that this image first appeared in Eisenstein's film of 1938.</li><li>Russian historiography has treated the battle as significant for the balance between Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a>, but whether it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and how large it was, is disputed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The earliest account of the battle, the Livonian Rhymed Chronicle, says the dead fell 'on the grass'; the Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, from the mid-15th century, was the first version to place the battle on the ice itself.</p></aside>
    </section>

